export const L5={title:'The physician–patient relationship, contracts and abandonment',hook:'A patient who keeps missing appointments receives a voicemail: “Don’t come back.” Another person collapses at a restaurant, and an off-duty nurse helps. Both situations raise the same question in different forms: when does a professional duty begin, and how may it properly end?',objectives:['Explain how a professional relationship and its duties are formed.','Distinguish express, implied and bilateral contracts in health care.','Describe patient rights and responsibilities within the relationship.','Apply proper termination steps and distinguish them from abandonment.','Describe the general purpose and limits of Good Samaritan laws.'],sections:[
['How the relationship begins',[
'A professional relationship usually begins when a clinician agrees, expressly or through conduct, to evaluate or treat a person. Scheduling an appointment, taking a history and recommending treatment are typical signs that a relationship exists. Once it does, the clinician owes duties such as competent care, appropriate confidentiality, truthful communication and continuity until the relationship ends properly. A casual comment at a social event or a general public lecture does not ordinarily create the same duty, although the facts and jurisdiction matter. Courts look at what actually happened, not only at labels.',
'Clinicians generally have discretion to decide whom they will accept as new patients, but that discretion is limited. A refusal cannot rest on unlawful discrimination, and covered hospitals have emergency obligations that apply regardless of an established relationship. Professional codes also discourage refusing care in ways that leave a person without reasonable access to necessary services. The beginning of the relationship matters because it defines when ordinary professional duties attach.']],
['Contracts: express, implied and bilateral',[
'A **contract** is an agreement that the law will enforce. An **express contract** is stated in words, spoken or written, such as a signed agreement for a specific procedure with stated fees. An **implied contract** is inferred from conduct: when a patient sits down, describes symptoms and the clinician examines them, both parties act as if an agreement exists even though no one wrote one. The HOSA sample question highlights this distinction. Most routine health care relationships rest largely on implied contracts supported by specific consents and financial agreements.',
'Health care contracts are typically **bilateral**: each side makes commitments. The clinician agrees to provide care that meets the applicable standard; the patient agrees to provide accurate information, follow the agreed plan or communicate disagreement, and pay for services under the applicable arrangement. A clinician should not promise a guaranteed cure, because such a promise can create an express warranty that care cannot honestly support. Contract elements commonly taught include an offer, acceptance, consideration (something of value exchanged), and parties with capacity to agree. A minor or a person lacking capacity may not be able to form every contract personally, which is one reason guardians and surrogates appear in health care.',
sort('Express or implied?',['Express contract','Implied contract'],[['A signed agreement describing a cosmetic procedure and its fee',0],['A verbal agreement to perform a stated test on Friday',0],['A patient rolls up a sleeve when a nurse approaches with a blood-pressure cuff',1],['A clinician examines a walk-in patient who describes symptoms',1],['A written financial agreement for a payment plan',0],['An unconscious patient treated in an emergency under an emergency doctrine',1]])]],
['Rights and responsibilities on both sides',[
'Patients generally have rights to information about their condition and care, to make informed decisions, to privacy and confidentiality, to access their records, to considerate and respectful treatment, and to refuse care within applicable law. Many organizations publish a patient bill of rights describing these expectations and the process for complaints. Rights operate within a relationship: they do not mean that every request must be granted, and they do not erase safety rules that protect other people.',
'Patients also carry responsibilities that make good care possible: providing complete and accurate health history, asking questions when they do not understand, following through on agreed plans or explaining why they cannot, keeping or canceling appointments, treating staff respectfully, and meeting financial obligations. Missing a responsibility does not cancel the clinician’s duties automatically. It is a reason to communicate, document and, if necessary, end the relationship properly.',
flip('Contract and relationship vocabulary',[['Implied contract','An agreement inferred from the conduct of the parties rather than stated words'],['Express contract','An agreement stated in spoken or written words'],['Consideration','Something of value exchanged in a contract'],['Bilateral contract','Both parties make promises to each other'],['Abandonment','Ending a care relationship without reasonable notice or arranging continued care'],['Good Samaritan law','State law protecting good-faith emergency helpers from some liability']])]],
['Ending the relationship properly',[
'Either party may end the relationship. A patient can simply leave or choose another clinician; good practice is to document the patient’s decision and send a letter confirming it. A clinician may end the relationship for reasons such as repeated missed appointments, refusal to follow an agreed plan without discussion, abusive behavior, or a change in practice, provided the reason is not discriminatory and the patient is not left in the middle of an acute need. The usual steps are a written notice, often sent by a method that confirms delivery, that states the end date, offers care during a reasonable transition period, explains how to obtain records and suggests how to find another clinician.',
'**Abandonment** occurs when a clinician ends the relationship without reasonable notice while the patient still needs care, or fails to arrange coverage when unavailable. Leaving a hospitalized patient without arranging another responsible clinician, or announcing “don’t come back” to a patient with an ongoing treatment need, can create liability and violates professional ethics. The opening voicemail is risky for both reasons: it gives no reasonable transition and no help locating continued care. Abandonment is different from a clinician’s vacation with arranged coverage, or a referral to a specialist with a clear handoff.',
order('End a care relationship without abandonment',['Identify a nondiscriminatory reason and check for any acute care need','Discuss the concern with the patient where appropriate','Send written notice with an effective date','Continue providing care during a reasonable transition period','Offer help finding another clinician and transfer records with authorization','Document the communication and the transition'])]],
['Emergencies outside the relationship',[
'**Good Samaritan laws**, which vary by state, generally protect people who give emergency help in good faith, without expectation of payment, from liability for ordinary negligence. They do not usually protect gross negligence or willful misconduct, and they often do not apply to professionals acting within their paid employment. Once a bystander begins helping, many legal frameworks expect them to continue until someone with equal or greater training takes over or until stopping is reasonable. Students should learn their own state’s rule rather than assuming one national standard.',
'Duty to act in an emergency is different inside a covered hospital. Federal emergency-screening obligations attach to the hospital, not to a Good Samaritan statute, and they apply whether or not a prior relationship exists. Keep the two ideas separate: one concerns protection for voluntary helpers; the other concerns obligations of covered institutions.',
kase('A missed-appointment pattern',{a:{text:'A patient with diabetes has missed four appointments in three months. A manager suggests deleting future appointments and telling the patient by voicemail not to return. What should happen first?',choices:[['Review the pattern, discuss barriers with the patient and document the conversation','b','Correct. Missed appointments may reflect transport, cost or scheduling barriers that can be addressed.',true],['Leave the voicemail today','b','A voicemail with no transition period and no help finding care risks abandonment.',false]]},b:{text:'After discussion, the pattern continues and the practice decides to end the relationship. Which notice is appropriate?',choices:[['A written letter with an end date, a reasonable period of continued care, help finding another clinician and a records process','c','Correct. This gives the patient time and a path to continued care.',true],['An immediate refusal to see the patient, effective today','c','Immediate termination while care is still needed risks abandonment.',false]]},c:{text:'The patient is seen during the transition period, receives medication refills and transfers records to a new clinic. The practice documents each step.',end:true}})]],
['Apply it to the opening cases',[
'For the patient told “don’t come back,” the practice should first explore and document the reasons for missed visits, then follow a written termination process if ending the relationship remains appropriate. For the restaurant emergency, the off-duty nurse acting voluntarily and in good faith is the classic situation Good Samaritan laws were written for, but the nurse should still act within their training, call for emergency services and hand over care properly. In both cases, the professional question is the same: identify whether a duty exists, act within it, and end it in a way that does not leave a person without needed care.']]
],takeaways:['Most health care relationships rest on implied, bilateral contracts supported by specific consents.','Terminate with written notice, a transition period and help finding care; anything less risks abandonment.','Good Samaritan laws vary by state and protect good-faith volunteers, not gross negligence.'],terms:[['Implied contract','An agreement inferred from conduct.'],['Abandonment','Ending care without reasonable notice or arrangements while care is still needed.'],['Good Samaritan law','State protection for good-faith emergency helpers.']],mcqs:[q('Which best describes an implied contract in health care?','An enforceable agreement shown by conduct without a specific written or spoken agreement','An agreement contracted through an independent agent','An agreement written in distinct, clear language','An agreement explained to the patient by the physician','Implied contracts arise from the parties’ actions.'),q('A patient signs a written agreement for a specific procedure and fee. This is an:','Express contract','Implied contract','Unenforceable promise','Advance directive','Express contracts are stated in words.'),q('Why should a clinician avoid promising a cure?','It may create an express warranty that care cannot honestly guarantee','Promises are illegal in every state','It cancels informed consent','It ends the implied contract','Guaranteed outcomes can become enforceable promises.'),q('Which action most clearly risks abandonment?','Ending care immediately while a patient still needs treatment, with no transition','Sending a written notice with 30 days of continued care','Arranging coverage during a vacation','Referring a patient with a clear handoff','Abandonment involves inadequate notice or arrangements.'),q('Good Samaritan laws generally protect:','Good-faith volunteers giving emergency help without expectation of payment','Gross negligence at the scene','Paid hospital staff on duty in every case','Any act performed in a hospital','Protection is limited and varies by state.'),q('Which is a patient responsibility?','Providing accurate health information','Guaranteeing a treatment outcome','Approving every staffing decision','Writing their own prescriptions','Accurate history supports safe care.'),q('A contract element meaning something of value exchanged is:','Consideration','Capacity','Precedent','Jurisdiction','Consideration is a classic contract element.'),q('A covered hospital’s emergency-screening duty differs from Good Samaritan protection because it:','Attaches to the institution regardless of a prior relationship','Applies only to off-duty volunteers','Protects gross negligence','Requires a signed contract first','The two concepts address different situations.')],frq:{prompt:'A practice wants to stop seeing a patient who has missed many appointments and still has an ongoing condition. Explain how the relationship was formed, what abandonment would look like, and the proper steps to end the relationship.',rubric:[['Explain relationship formation and implied contract',['implied','conduct','relationship','contract']],['Define abandonment in context',['abandon','notice','transition','still needs']],['List proper termination steps',['written','letter','reasonable','records','another clinician']],['Address barriers and documentation',['barrier','discuss','document']]],model:'The relationship formed through conduct when the practice accepted, examined and treated the patient, creating an implied, bilateral contract with ongoing duties. Abandonment would occur if the practice ended care without reasonable notice while the patient still needed treatment, for example by refusing all appointments immediately. The practice should first discuss the missed visits to identify barriers and document the conversation. If ending the relationship remains appropriate and nondiscriminatory, it should send a written notice with an effective date, continue care during a reasonable transition period, help the patient find another clinician, transfer records with authorization, and document each step.'},sources:[RULES,AMA_TERM,AMA_REL]};

export const L6={title:'Medical records, confidentiality and HIPAA',hook:'A receptionist recognizes a neighbor’s name on the schedule and mentions it to a friend. A patient asks for a copy of their chart and is told records “belong to the hospital.” A laptop with unencrypted patient files is stolen from a car. Each scenario involves a different rule about who may see, keep and protect health information.',objectives:['Describe the purposes, ownership and correction of medical records.','Explain the HIPAA Privacy Rule, protected health information and minimum necessary standard.','Distinguish uses and disclosures that need authorization from those that do not.','Describe patient rights, the Security Rule and breach notification.','Recognize records that carry special protections.'],sections:[
['Why records matter and who owns them',[
'The medical record documents the patient’s history, findings, decisions and care. It supports continuity among clinicians, communicates plans, supports billing, provides evidence in legal proceedings and supplies data for quality improvement and research. A record that is incomplete, late or altered weakens all of these purposes. In litigation, the guiding saying is that care not documented may be argued as care not done, which is an overstatement but explains why accurate, timely entries matter.',
'The physical or electronic record generally belongs to the provider or facility that created it, but the **information** belongs in an important sense to the patient: patients have legal rights to access it, obtain copies and request amendments. Telling a patient that records simply “belong to the hospital” confuses custody with access. Retention periods are set by state law and other requirements and vary, often longer for minors. Records must be stored, retained and eventually destroyed securely according to those rules.',
'Corrections preserve the original. In a paper record, the classic method is a single line through the error so it remains readable, the correction, the date, the time and the writer’s initials, with a reason if policy requires. Electronic records keep an audit trail of amendments. Never erase, obliterate, back-date or add a late entry disguised as a timely one; a clearly labeled late entry is acceptable when policy allows.']],
['HIPAA and protected health information',[
'The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 led to national standards for health information. The **Privacy Rule** applies to **covered entities**—health plans, health care clearinghouses and health care providers who conduct certain transactions electronically—and to their **business associates** that handle protected information for them. **Protected health information (PHI)** is individually identifiable health information held or transmitted by a covered entity or business associate, in any form: paper, electronic or spoken. A name on a schedule linked to a clinic visit is PHI.',
'The **minimum necessary** standard requires reasonable efforts to use or disclose only the information needed for a purpose. It does not apply to disclosures to a provider for treatment, because clinicians may need the full picture. Inside an organization, role-based access should match job duties. The receptionist in the opening case had access for scheduling, not for conversation with friends; curiosity is not a permitted purpose, even if the software allows the view. HIPAA sets a federal floor; state laws that are more protective of privacy generally still apply.',
sort('Is authorization needed?',['Permitted without the patient’s authorization','Generally requires written authorization'],[['Sending records to a specialist for treatment',0],['Submitting a claim for payment',0],['Internal quality review of care',0],['Required public-health reporting of a reportable disease',0],['Giving a patient list to a marketing company',1],['Releasing psychotherapy notes to an employer',1],['Sharing a diagnosis with the patient’s employer for hiring decisions',1]])]],
['Uses, disclosures and authorization',[
'Covered entities may use and disclose PHI without authorization for **treatment, payment and health care operations**, often abbreviated **TPO**. The Privacy Rule also permits certain disclosures for public interest purposes, such as required reporting of disease, abuse or neglect, health oversight, some law enforcement purposes, judicial proceedings under specified conditions, and to avert a serious threat to health or safety, each with conditions. A subpoena signed by an attorney is not the same as a court order; organizations follow specific procedures before responding.',
'Most other uses, including most marketing and the sale of PHI, require a written **authorization** that specifies what information, to whom, for what purpose and for how long, and that the patient may revoke. **Psychotherapy notes** kept separate from the medical record have extra protection. Patients receive a **Notice of Privacy Practices** explaining how their information may be used and their rights. Family involvement is allowed in many situations when the patient agrees or does not object, or when professional judgment supports it in the patient’s best interest, but it should follow the patient’s expressed wishes.']],
['Patient rights under the Privacy Rule',[
'Patients have the right to inspect and obtain a copy of their records in a designated record set, generally within 30 days of a request, with one possible 30-day extension, and covered entities may charge only a reasonable, cost-based fee. Patients may request an amendment; the entity may deny it with a written explanation, and the patient may submit a statement of disagreement. Patients may receive an accounting of certain disclosures, request restrictions, and request confidential communications such as contact at an alternate address. Patients who pay in full out of pocket can restrict disclosure of that service to their health plan.',
match('Match the privacy term',[['Health plan, clearinghouse or electronic-transacting provider','Covered entity'],['Vendor handling PHI on behalf of a covered entity','Business associate'],['Use only what the purpose requires','Minimum necessary'],['Treatment, payment and operations','TPO'],['Document explaining privacy practices and rights','Notice of Privacy Practices'],['Written permission for a specific disclosure','Authorization']])]],
['Security, breaches and special records',[
'The **Security Rule** protects electronic PHI through administrative safeguards (risk analysis, training, policies), physical safeguards (facility access, device controls) and technical safeguards (access control, audit logs, encryption, transmission security). A **breach** is an impermissible use or disclosure that compromises PHI unless a risk assessment shows a low probability of compromise. Under the **Breach Notification Rule**, affected individuals must be notified without unreasonable delay and no later than 60 days after discovery. Breaches affecting 500 or more people also require notice to HHS and, for residents of a state or jurisdiction, to prominent media; smaller breaches are logged and reported to HHS annually. Properly encrypted data that is lost is generally not a reportable breach, which is why encryption matters for the stolen laptop.',
'Some records carry additional protection. Federal rules give certain substance use disorder treatment records stronger confidentiality. State laws often add protection for HIV status, mental health and genetic information, and for minors who may consent to particular services. Penalties for HIPAA violations range from civil fines, scaled by culpability, to criminal penalties for knowingly obtaining or disclosing PHI improperly.',
{t:'w',kind:'ethicslab',title:'Reason through a privacy decision',scenario:1}]],
['Resolve the three opening cases',[
'The receptionist disclosed PHI without a permitted purpose; the organization should investigate, apply its sanctions policy, retrain staff and assess whether the disclosure is a reportable breach. The patient requesting records has a right of access within the required time frame, subject to limited exceptions; the hospital owns the physical record but cannot use ownership to deny lawful access. The stolen unencrypted laptop requires a breach risk assessment and likely notification, along with corrective steps such as encryption and device policies. In every case, the guiding questions are who the information belongs to in practice, what purpose justifies its use, and what safeguards protect it.']]
],takeaways:['The facility owns the record, but the patient has strong rights to access and amend the information.','TPO uses do not need authorization; marketing and most other uses do; always apply minimum necessary outside treatment.','Encrypt devices, notify affected individuals within 60 days of discovering a breach, and correct records without erasing originals.'],terms:[['PHI','Individually identifiable health information held or transmitted by a covered entity or business associate.'],['Minimum necessary','Limit uses and disclosures to the information needed for the purpose.'],['Breach','An impermissible use or disclosure that compromises PHI.']],mcqs:[q('Who owns the physical or electronic medical record?','The provider or facility, while the patient has rights of access','The patient exclusively','The insurer','The state licensing board','Custody and access are different concepts.'),q('Which disclosure is permitted without patient authorization?','Sending records to a consulting specialist for treatment','Selling a patient list to a marketer','Telling a friend about a neighbor’s visit','Posting a photo of a patient online','Treatment is part of TPO.'),q('The minimum necessary standard does NOT apply to:','Disclosures to a provider for treatment','Billing staff reviewing an account','Responding to a records request from a researcher','Internal audits','Treating clinicians may need complete information.'),q('How should a paper charting error be corrected?','Draw a single line through it, write the correction, date, time and initials','Use correction fluid','Tear out the page','Rewrite the whole page and discard the original','Corrections must preserve the original entry.'),q('Generally, how long does a covered entity have to act on a patient’s request for access?','30 days, with one possible 30-day extension','24 hours','1 year','There is no time limit','HHS guidance sets the access time frame.'),q('A breach affecting 500 or more residents of a state requires notification to individuals, HHS and:','Prominent media serving that area','The patient’s employer','Every hospital in the country','No one else','Large breaches require media notice.'),q('Which safeguard is technical under the Security Rule?','Encryption and audit logs','Workforce training','Locked server room doors','A sanctions policy','Technical safeguards involve technology controls.'),q('Viewing a neighbor’s chart out of curiosity is:','An impermissible use, even if the software allows access','Permitted because the employee is staff','Required for quality improvement','Allowed if no one finds out','Access must match a permitted job purpose.')],frq:{prompt:'An employee views a celebrity patient’s chart without a work reason, and the same week a laptop with unencrypted patient files is stolen. Explain which HIPAA rules apply, whether each event may be a breach, and the organization’s required and recommended actions.',rubric:[['Explain PHI, permitted purposes and minimum necessary',['phi','purpose','minimum necessary','permitted']],['Analyze breach status and notification timing',['breach','risk assessment','60 days','notify']],['Identify safeguards (encryption, access control, audit)',['encrypt','access','audit','safeguard']],['Describe sanctions, training and corrective action',['sanction','training','policy','correct']]],model:'Both events involve protected health information. The employee’s curiosity access is an impermissible use because it has no treatment, payment or operations purpose and violates minimum necessary role-based access; audit logs should detect it. The stolen laptop exposes unencrypted electronic PHI. For each event the organization performs a breach risk assessment; unless it shows a low probability of compromise, affected individuals must be notified without unreasonable delay and within 60 days of discovery, and large breaches also require notice to HHS and media. The organization should sanction the employee under its policy, retrain staff, review access controls and audit monitoring, encrypt portable devices, and document corrective actions under the Security Rule’s administrative, physical and technical safeguards.'},sources:[RULES,PRIV,ACCESS,BREACH]};

export const L7={title:'Workplace law, employment practices and cultural perspective',hook:'A phlebotomist sustains a needlestick. A pregnant technician asks about leave. A manager wants to hire “someone younger with more energy.” An interpreter is unavailable, so a patient’s teenage child is asked to translate a cancer diagnosis. Health care workplaces are governed by laws that protect both workers and patients.',objectives:['Describe major federal employment laws and whom they protect.','Explain OSHA’s Bloodborne Pathogens Standard and worker safety duties.','Recognize harassment and discrimination in hiring and employment.','Explain language access and culturally responsive communication obligations.','Apply workplace law to realistic scenarios.'],sections:[
['Employment relationships and at-will work',[
'Most U.S. employment is **at will**: either the employer or employee can end the relationship at any time for any lawful reason or no reason. The key word is lawful. An employer cannot fire someone for a reason prohibited by statute, such as race or disability, or in retaliation for reporting safety violations, filing a discrimination complaint or taking protected leave. Contracts, union agreements and state laws can add protections. Employers must also verify that new hires are authorized to work, and many health care roles require license verification, background checks and credentialing before work begins.',
'Hiring practices must focus on job-related qualifications. Interview questions about age, pregnancy plans, religion, national origin or disability can suggest discriminatory intent. The manager in the opening case who wants “someone younger” is describing age as a hiring factor, which federal law prohibits for applicants 40 and older at covered employers.']],
['Federal anti-discrimination laws',[
'**Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964** prohibits employment discrimination based on race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy, sexual orientation and gender identity) and national origin, generally for employers with 15 or more employees. The **Age Discrimination in Employment Act** protects people 40 and older, generally at employers with 20 or more employees. The **Americans with Disabilities Act** prohibits disability discrimination and requires **reasonable accommodation** for qualified individuals unless it would cause undue hardship. The **Equal Pay Act** requires equal pay for substantially equal work regardless of sex. The **Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act** bars employers from using genetic information in employment decisions. The **Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC)** enforces these laws, and retaliation against someone who complains is itself prohibited.',
'**Sexual harassment** includes **quid pro quo** harassment, where job benefits depend on submitting to sexual conduct, and a **hostile work environment**, where unwelcome conduct is severe or pervasive enough to alter working conditions. Employers should have clear policies, accessible reporting channels and prompt investigations.',
'Under the ADA, accommodation is worked out through an **interactive process**: the employee asks for help related to a disability, the employer and employee discuss the job’s essential functions and possible changes, and the employer chooses an effective option that does not cause undue hardship. Examples include modified schedules, ergonomic equipment, reassignment to a vacant position or adjusted lifting duties. An employer may not ask applicants about disabilities before a conditional job offer, but may ask whether they can perform essential job functions. A medical examination after an offer must be required of everyone entering the same job category, and the results are kept confidential and separate from personnel files.',
match('Match the law to its protection',[['Race, color, religion, sex, national origin','Title VII'],['Workers 40 and older','ADEA'],['Disability and reasonable accommodation','ADA'],['Equal pay for equal work regardless of sex','Equal Pay Act'],['Genetic information in employment','GINA'],['Job-protected leave for serious health conditions','FMLA']])]],
['Leave, wages and compensation for injury',[
'The **Family and Medical Leave Act** gives eligible employees of covered employers (generally 50 or more employees) up to 12 workweeks of unpaid, job-protected leave in a 12-month period for reasons such as the birth or placement of a child, the employee’s own serious health condition, or care of a spouse, child or parent with a serious health condition, with group health coverage maintained. The **Fair Labor Standards Act** sets federal minimum wage and overtime rules for nonexempt employees. **Workers’ compensation**, a state-based system, provides medical care and wage replacement for work-related injuries, generally without requiring the worker to prove employer fault, and in exchange limits lawsuits against the employer.']],
['Worker safety and OSHA',[
'The **Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA)** requires employers to provide a workplace free of recognized serious hazards. Its **Bloodborne Pathogens Standard** requires a written **exposure control plan**, training, engineering controls such as safer sharps devices and sharps containers, work-practice controls, personal protective equipment at no cost, the **hepatitis B vaccine offered at no cost** to employees with occupational exposure, and post-exposure evaluation and follow-up. The **Hazard Communication Standard** requires labeling and **Safety Data Sheets** for hazardous chemicals. Workers have the right to report hazards without retaliation.',
'The **Needlestick Safety and Prevention Act of 2000** strengthened the standard by requiring employers to evaluate and adopt safer medical devices, such as retractable needles and needleless IV connectors, and to involve frontline workers in choosing them. Employers must also keep a **sharps injury log**. Health care workers face high rates of workplace violence, especially in emergency departments and behavioral health units, so many organizations use violence-prevention programs, de-escalation training and reporting systems. Safe patient handling programs reduce back injuries from lifting. These programs reflect a broader principle: protecting staff is part of protecting patients, because injured or fatigued workers make more errors.',
order('Respond to a needlestick injury',['Wash the site with soap and water','Report the exposure to a supervisor immediately','Go for prompt post-exposure medical evaluation','Document the incident and the source if known','Receive follow-up testing and counseling as directed','Review how the injury happened to prevent recurrence'])]],
['Cultural perspective and language access',[
'Health care workers serve patients whose language, culture, religion and beliefs shape how they understand illness and make decisions. **Cultural humility** means approaching each person as the expert on their own life, asking about preferences rather than assuming them from group membership, and examining one’s own biases. Stereotyping, even when well-intended, can lead to the wrong questions and the wrong plan. Implicit bias can influence pain assessment, communication time and referrals without conscious intent, which is why organizations monitor outcomes and train staff.',
'**Title VI of the Civil Rights Act** prohibits national-origin discrimination in federally funded programs, which includes providing meaningful access for people with limited English proficiency. Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act applies nondiscrimination requirements to many health programs. In practice this means qualified medical interpreters, translated vital documents and accommodations such as sign-language interpreters under disability law. Family members—especially children—should not be relied on to interpret sensitive information except in limited, urgent circumstances or when the patient knowingly prefers it, because errors, omissions and emotional burden are common.',
kase('An interpreter is unavailable',{a:{text:'A Spanish-speaking patient is about to hear a new cancer diagnosis. The in-person interpreter is delayed, and a nurse suggests the patient’s 14-year-old son translate. What is the best response?',choices:[['Use a qualified phone or video interpreter now','b','Correct. Remote qualified interpreters provide accurate, confidential interpretation.',true],['Let the son translate to save time','b','A child interpreting a serious diagnosis risks errors and places an inappropriate burden on him.',false]]},b:{text:'The patient asks that her son be present but not interpret. How should the team proceed?',choices:[['Respect her preference, use the qualified interpreter and document the interpreter’s ID','c','Correct. The patient chooses who is present; accuracy comes from a qualified interpreter.',true],['Ask the son to leave against her wishes','c','The patient may choose to have family present for support.',false]]},c:{text:'The diagnosis is explained through a qualified interpreter with teach-back to check understanding. The interpreter’s identification number and the patient’s preferences are documented.',end:true}})]],
['Apply the laws to the opening cases',[
'The phlebotomist should follow the exposure plan immediately; the employer must provide evaluation and follow-up and review sharps safety. The pregnant technician may be eligible for FMLA leave and is protected from pregnancy discrimination under Title VII; additional federal and state laws may require reasonable accommodations. The hiring manager must evaluate candidates on job-related qualifications, not age. The diagnosis should be delivered through a qualified interpreter. Across all four, documentation, clear policies and non-retaliation protect both staff and patients.']]
],takeaways:['At-will employment still forbids firing for unlawful reasons or retaliation.','Title VII, ADEA (40+), ADA, Equal Pay Act, GINA and FMLA protect workers; the EEOC enforces most anti-discrimination laws.','OSHA requires an exposure control plan and free hepatitis B vaccination; use qualified interpreters, not children, for clinical communication.'],terms:[['At-will employment','Employment either side may end for any lawful reason.'],['Reasonable accommodation','A change that enables a qualified person with a disability to do the job.'],['Cultural humility','Ongoing self-reflection and curiosity about each patient’s own beliefs and preferences.']],mcqs:[q('Which law protects workers 40 and older from age discrimination?','Age Discrimination in Employment Act','Equal Pay Act','FMLA','Title VI','The ADEA covers workers 40 and older.'),q('Under FMLA, an eligible employee may take up to:','12 workweeks of unpaid, job-protected leave in a 12-month period','12 months of paid leave','6 weeks of paid leave only for illness','Unlimited leave','FMLA leave is unpaid and job-protected.'),q('OSHA’s Bloodborne Pathogens Standard requires employers to:','Offer the hepatitis B vaccine at no cost to exposed employees','Require employees to buy their own gloves','Test every patient for HIV','Ban all needles','The vaccine must be offered free of charge.'),q('A supervisor offers a promotion in exchange for dates. This is:','Quid pro quo harassment','A hostile environment only if repeated daily','Permitted if the employee agrees','An FMLA violation','Quid pro quo ties job benefits to sexual conduct.'),q('Which agency enforces most federal anti-discrimination employment laws?','EEOC','OSHA','FDA','CMS','The Equal Employment Opportunity Commission enforces these laws.'),q('Why should a child not interpret a serious diagnosis?','Accuracy, confidentiality and emotional burden concerns','Children cannot speak two languages','It is always a crime','Interpreters are optional for all patients','Qualified interpreters are the safer standard.'),q('Workers’ compensation generally:','Pays for work-related injuries without requiring proof of employer fault','Requires suing the employer','Covers only injuries outside work','Is a federal insurance program for all workers','It is a state-based no-fault system.'),q('Cultural humility emphasizes:','Asking about each person’s preferences and examining one’s own biases','Predicting beliefs from ethnicity','Avoiding all discussion of culture','Using family members as interpreters','Individuals are experts on their own beliefs.')],frq:{prompt:'A hospital unit faces a needlestick injury, a hiring manager who prefers younger candidates, and a patient with limited English proficiency who needs to give consent. Identify the applicable laws or standards and the correct response for each.',rubric:[['Needlestick: OSHA standard and response',['osha','bloodborne','exposure','report','follow-up']],['Hiring: age discrimination law',['adea','40','age','qualification']],['Language access: Title VI and interpreters',['interpreter','title vi','limited english','qualified']],['Documentation and non-retaliation',['document','retaliat','policy']]],model:'The needlestick falls under OSHA’s Bloodborne Pathogens Standard: the worker washes the site, reports immediately and receives prompt post-exposure evaluation and follow-up under the employer’s exposure control plan, and the employer reviews sharps safety. Preferring younger candidates violates the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, which protects applicants 40 and older; hiring must rest on job-related qualifications. For the patient with limited English proficiency, Title VI and Section 1557 require meaningful language access, so consent should be obtained through a qualified medical interpreter, not a family member or child, with teach-back to confirm understanding. The unit documents each event, follows policy and must not retaliate against anyone who reports an injury or a discrimination concern.'},sources:[RULES,EEOC,OSHA_BBP,DOL_FMLA,HHS_LEP]};
